        "date":  "2026-08-31",
        "theme":  "Strategy",
        "summary":  "Neoclouds — GPU-native providers like CoreWeave, Lambda, and Vultr — achieved $25B+ in revenue in 2025 and offer AI compute at roughly one-third the cost of hyperscalers, with faster provisioning and bare-metal GPU access optimized for large-scale training and inference. However, enterprises report significant trade-offs: neoclouds lack the managed services, compliance certifications, identity controls, audit logging, and enterprise SLAs that hyperscalers provide. Ironically, hyperscalers are neoclouds\u0027 biggest customers — Microsoft accounted for 67% of CoreWeave\u0027s 2025 revenue — and hyperscaler custom silicon is eroding the cost advantage neoclouds currently hold.",
        "execSummary":  "The neocloud versus hyperscaler debate matters for every enterprise CIO setting AI infrastructure strategy: neoclouds offer 60-70% GPU cost savings and faster provisioning for frontier training, but enterprise buyers consistently report that governance, security, identity, and SLA maturity still sit decisively with hyperscalers. The most likely outcome is coexistence — neoclouds win large-scale model training while hyperscalers retain regulated workloads — meaning most organizations will need a deliberate multi-cloud AI infrastructure strategy rather than an all-in bet on either category. Key risk factors for neocloud commitments include high customer concentration (CoreWeave\u0027s top two clients represent 77% of revenue) and GPU depreciation cycles of four to five years. Before adopting a neocloud, IT leaders should validate exit portability, security certification coverage, and whether their AI workloads are truly training-bound or primarily inference.",
